==English==
{{was wotd|2023|July|15}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|acid|test|notext=1|type=exocentric}}; {{senseno|en|chemical test with acid}} (“test involving the reaction of an acid with another chemical”) and {{senseno|en|rigorous test, figuratively}} (“rigorous test or appraisal of the quality or worth of something”) refer to the use of [[nitric acid#Noun|nitric acid]] to test if a metal is [[gold#Noun|gold]]; true gold is not affected by the acid.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=271645|date=March 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

{{senseno|en|LSD party|uc=1}} (“party based around the use and advocacy of the psychedelic drug LSD”) is a pun referencing the slang term {{m|en|acid|t=LSD}}. The original “acid test” parties were thrown by the {{w|Merry Pranksters}} group between 1965 and 1966 and advertised by the slogan “Can you pass the acid test?”<ref>{{R:Greens|id=loxfqoi#nliz5gi}}</ref><ref>{{cite-journal|en|author=Jesse Jarnow|title=Acid Tests Turn 50: Wavy Gravy, Merry Prankster Ken Babbs Look Back|work={{w|Rolling Stone}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230315053455/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-news/acid-tests-turn-50-wavy-gravy-merry-prankster-ken-babbs-look-back-57641/|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Penske Media Corporation}}|date=30 November 2015|issn=0035-791X|oclc=1787396}}.</ref>

